german chancellor angela merkel has  defended her handling of the pandemic in  a speech  in germany's parliament she said that  lockdown measures  would have been effective but new  variants of the coronavirus mean  infection numbers are rising across  europe she said that germany is  currently relying on mass  testing to try to control its third wave  of infections  and urge germans to take up the offer of  free rapid testing frustration  has been growing over the slow vaccine  rollout  but merkel said it was right that the eu  decided to acquire  and authorize vaccines jointly despite  all the difficulties it was right  decide to jointly acquire and authorize  vaccines in the european union we see  there are some slight differences  in the distribution and there are huge  debates about this but what would happen  if some of the countries had a vaccine  others did not i cannot imagine it  because it would shake the very  foundations  of the union  well joining me now are chief political  correspondent melody crane here in the  studio and our political correspondent  thomas sparrow is down  at the german bonus title let's start  with you melinda we heard angela merkel  there defending the eu  joint procurement policy when it deals  to vaccines did she  offer a convincing defense of that what  do you make of it  i think she's right that had germany  gone it alone  at that stage it would indeed have  shaken europe to its very foundations  because we would have seen  some powerful countries like germany  with adequate vaccine  and then undoubtedly others smaller  countries that don't have a lot of  bargaining power in international  pharma markets uh possibly without  vaccine so i think she's right on that  point  the problem is brussels did not  adequately go  into crisis mode when they then began to  look at negotiations with the big  drug makers they essentially haggled  over  price over liability rather than seeing  that in a crisis  time is of the absolute essence  and germany also bears some  responsibility here because germany  as europe's biggest economic power did  not  throw the full force of its expertise  and negotiating skills  at brussels disposal essentially germany  stood back  and let brussels handle this in a  rather typically brussels bureaucratic  fashion and so i think  if we look at the procurement process  mistakes were made  and berlin does ultimately also bear  some responsibility for that  and it should be said that uh that  germany had the eu  presidency when much of this was being  negotiated um  thomas it's the the slow rollout of the  vaccine  is of course an issue but it's not the  only thing angel america talked about  in her in her speech today uh she's  being criticized for the government's  handling of the pandemic in general how  did  she respond to that criticism in her  speech  angela merkel stressed once again as she  did earlier in the week that this is a  new kind of pandemic in particular  because of the  more contagious coronavirus variants but  at the same time she stressed that  germany now has at its disposal  other elements to fight that new  pandemic as  if you compare it with the earlier  stages of the coronavirus pandemic and  she specifically mentioned  testing you mentioned that as well terry  she mentioned again germany's vaccine  rollout which  has been very slow but is progressing  she mentioned the fact that in april  germany will receive a high number of  vaccines which will help to speed up the  process as well as the inclusion of gps  and family doctors in the whole vaccine  distribution and she also once again  defended the use of the coronavirus  warn app which was very useful for  germany in the first wave of the  pandemic in particular for its track and  trace  policy but which has been criticized in  recent months  for people who consider it not very  useful when it comes to germany's fight  against the coroner virus angela merkel  also stressed that it is not possible to  simply only highlight the negative  aspects of  policy and that's why she also focus on  those important issues that germany has  at its disposal to fight the coronavirus  pandemic  did the chancellor offer any new ideas  thomas for  how to deal with the rising infection  numbers  terry no because i don't think this is  the venue for angela merkel to present  new ideas these kinds of government  speeches ahead of  eu summits are normally intended to  present broader elements of policy  which can then lead to a discussion here  in in parliament  so as such what we heard today from  angela merkel was some of those key  elements that the government both  regional and federal governments have  put in practice in recent months to try  and fight  the pandemic angela merkel once again  stressed that cautious reopening plan  that was agreed on earlier  this month in one of those 20 meetings  that have taken place as the coronavirus  pandemic began at  the german chancery between regional and  federal leaders and that cautious  reopening plan is one  that she again defended as part of  germany's overall policy to fight the  pandemic  thomas thank you very much that was  thomas sparrow down at the bundestag  and here in the studio our chief  political correspondent melinda crane  thank you very much  you
 

